HDMI QP FRL link-training spike for Orange Pi 5B.

Not part of the shipped patch queue. This patch answers one question and
nothing else: can the RK3588S HDMI TX train a Fixed Rate Link with the
attached sink?

It adds LTS1..LTS3 of the HDMI 2.1 link training state machine to the
DW HDMI QP bridge, and a sysfs trigger in the Rockchip glue that switches
the PHY into FRL mode and runs training once, on whatever mode is already
active. Nothing runs at boot; the default modeset path is untouched, so a
failed experiment cannot take the display down before login.

The trigger is one-way. After it runs, the next atomic_check fails with
-EINVAL (DRM still asks the PHY for a TMDS char rate while the PHY is in
FRL mode) and the CRTC is torn down. Recovery is a reboot. Do not try to
undo it by poking the controller: by then it is unclocked, and the register
access hangs the SoC hard with nothing in the log.

Deliberately not implemented: LTS4 rate renegotiation, TxFFE adjustment,
the LTSP supervision loop, audio, DSC, and any DRM core integration.

Result on an Orange Pi 5B + Dell U2725QE, 2026-09-07, from a live
3840x2160@60 RGB 8-bit mode:

  FRL3 (4 x 6 Gbps, 24 Gbps)   PASS on the first attempt. The sink requested
                               LTP patterns 5/6/7/8 and reported all four
                               lanes trained after a single round, with TxFFE
                               left at level 0.
  FRL4 (4 x 8 Gbps, 32 Gbps)   FAIL: DDC NACK ~51ms after the first LTP write.
  FRL5 (4 x 10 Gbps, 40 Gbps)  FAIL: identical.
  FRL6 (4 x 12 Gbps, 48 Gbps)  FAIL: identical.

The ceiling sits between 24 and 32 Gbps. Untested whether that is the cable,
the missing TxFFE, or the PHY's LCPLL setup above 2.4 GHz - no upstream driver
has ever exercised the FRL path in phy-rockchip-samsung-hdptx, so it is not a
well-trodden road.

